<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry IV of Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1425–<a href="/years/1474">1474</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trastamara">House of Trastámara</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry IV was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> from 1454 to 1474. His reign was marked by noble rebellion and disputes over the legitimacy of his daughter Joanna, and he was briefly offered the crown of Catalonia in 1462. His half-sister Isabella succeeded him after a war of succession.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-castile.jpg" alt="Henry IV of Castile" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg von Ehingen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ENRIQUEIV.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was born on 5 January 1425 in Valladolid, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-castile">John II of Castile</a> and Maria of Aragon. He displaced his older sister Eleanor as heir apparent and became Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>. In his youth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was controlled by Álvaro de Luna, who chose his companions and directed his education. One of them, Juan Pacheco, became his closest confidant. In 1440, at fifteen, he married Blanche II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. In 1445 he won the First Battle of Olmedo against the Infantes of Aragon, after which Luna's power waned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>John II died on 20 July 1454, and Henry was proclaimed king the next day. In 1455 he married Joan of Portugal, sister of Afonso V, to secure an alliance with Portugal. He convened the Cuéllar Courts to launch an offensive against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, but the campaigns of 1455 and 1458 became a war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> and were unpopular. Pacheco, by then Marquis of Villena, and his brother Pedro Girón handled much of the government, and Henry took other advisers, among them Beltrán de la Cueva, to balance them.</p><p>Nobles formed a League of Nobles in March 1460. On 11 August 1462 Catalonia elected Henry Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, but his intervention failed and he agreed to the Judgment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a>, abandoning the Catalans. Queen Joan gave birth to a daughter, Joanna, in 1462, and rebels doubted her paternity and called her la Beltraneja. Under pressure from the league, Henry at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> in 1464 disowned Joanna and named his half-brother Alfonso heir. He later went back on this, and at the Farce of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a> the nobles deposed him in effigy and crowned Alfonso as a rival king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alfonso died in 1468, and his sister Isabella took his place as the rebels' champion. At Toros de Guisando Henry agreed to exclude Joanna from the succession and recognise Isabella as heir. He died on 11 December 1474 and was buried at Santa María de Guadalupe. A war between Joanna, supported by Portugal, and Isabella, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, followed, and Isabella won.</p><p>Henry was nicknamed the Impotent, and contemporaries thought him impotent or homosexual. Historians note that the royal chronicles were written or revised under Isabella, who had a strong interest in proving Joanna illegitimate, so Joanna's paternity cannot be settled. During his reign the nobles grew more powerful and the nation less centralised.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry IV of Castile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Near Olmedo in Castile, the forces of <b>Henry IV of Castile</b> met those of his half-brother Alfonso, Prince of Asturias, in their succession war.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-20">20 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Casa de las Aldabas, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.65,-4.73&amp;year=1456" aria-label="Casa de las Aldabas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4435 680 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry IV of Castile"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4885 950h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4885 950h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.65,-4.73&amp;year=1456">What was here in 1456? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
